# SABOTAGE: T166 / ADR-050 §4.5 — the fail-closed arm of the public status page
# keeps its honest BODY and loses its honest STATUS CODE: a substrate it cannot read
# is served as `200` carrying the "temporarily unavailable" page instead of `503`.
# This is the fail-honest lie in its most plausible form, because it looks like a
# UX improvement — a human opening the page reads the truth either way, and the
# maintainer can argue the page "rendered fine". What breaks is every reader that is
# not a human: an uptime checker, a load balancer, a synthetic monitor and a status
# aggregator all read the code, not the prose, so a cockpit whose own status source
# is down reports itself green for as long as the outage lasts.
#
# Flips the named §4.5 red (an unreadable substrate must be a 503 that SAYS it is
# unavailable). Everything else stays GREEN: the 200 control, the opt-in reds, both
# masking reds, the rate-limit red and the no-namespace arm's own refusal all still
# pass — so the red fails on the status code alone, which is exactly the claim.
#
# NOT TOUCHED, deliberately: `unavailable_page/1` and its wording (the point is that
# the body stays truthful while the code lies), the `{:error, :rate_limited}` arm
# above it, `Samen.Fleet.PublicStatus.read/2`'s own `{:error, :unavailable}` return
# and its `rescue` (the projection is still honest; only the HTTP framing lies), and
# the 200 success path.
#
# Changes ONE integer literal, so nothing goes unused and the file still compiles
# under `--warnings-as-errors`.
# APP: samen_web
# TEST_FILES: test/samen/web/fleet_public_status_test.exs
# MUST_FAIL: an unreadable substrate -> 503 that SAYS it is unavailable
diff --git a/samen_web/lib/samen/web/fleet/public_status.ex b/samen_web/lib/samen/web/fleet/public_status.ex
--- a/samen_web/lib/samen/web/fleet/public_status.ex
+++ b/samen_web/lib/samen/web/fleet/public_status.ex
@@ -80,7 +80,10 @@ defmodule Samen.Web.Fleet.PublicStatus do
         |> send_resp(429, "")
 
       {:error, _reason} ->
-        html(conn, 503, unavailable_page(title(opts)))
+        # SABOTAGE (T166/ADR-050 §4.5): the fail-closed STATUS CODE becomes 200. The
+        # body still says "unavailable", so a human sees the truth and every monitor
+        # reading only the HTTP code sees green.
+        html(conn, 200, unavailable_page(title(opts)))
     end
   end
 
